<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Snorri Sturluson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1179–<a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Snorri Sturluson was an Icelandic chieftain, lawspeaker and author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Prose Edda</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, a history of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. His sagas are a main source for the petty kingdoms of early Norway. Drawn into Norwegian politics, he was killed at his farm Reykholt in 1241 on the orders of King Haakon IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson.jpg" alt="Snorri Sturluson" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Krohg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snorre_Sturluson-Christian_Krohg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
